        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: preserve embedded prompt caching across room events and native Responses",
        "body": "Related: #102175\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where long-lived embedded sessions could lose prompt-cache continuity when ordinary turns, ambient group `room_event` turns, queued work, compaction, and native Responses continuation shared one session.\n\nThe failure had three connected causes:\n\n- turn-specific reply delivery leaked into cache-visible prompt and tool construction;\n- owner/sender checks changed the prompt-visible tool catalogue from 47 schemas to 44 on some turns;\n- queue, memory, compaction, and provider-reconstruction paths independently rebuilt state, while OpenClaw's built-in authenticated Responses stream could miss native delta continuation.\n\nStrict room delivery and sender authorization are still required. The bug was making those current-turn decisions mutate reusable prompt prefixes and tool schemas.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis PR separates stable model context from current-turn behavior:\n\n- Actual source-reply delivery remains independent from the session-stable prompt-facing mode. Executable `message` tools receive the real turn mode, including the strict room-event source-reply sink.\n- The model sees one stable session/runtime tool catalogue. `cron`, `gateway`, `nodes`, and `computer` are no longer removed merely because sender or event policy changes.\n- One trusted `ToolAccessPolicy` resolver derives a deterministic full policy snapshot and drives the hard runtime gate. Unavailable protected tools return `TOOL_ACCESS_DENIED`, marked non-retryable, before hooks or implementations can run.\n- Sender trust is explicit: owners and runtime-owned `cron`, `heartbeat`, and `memory` turns are distinct from non-owner and genuinely unknown senders. Explicit non-owner identity and ambient `room_event` policy override an internal trigger.\n- Full policy snapshots are inserted before the current user content when permissions or provider route change, or when queueing, memory work, compaction, reconstruction, recovery, or fallback makes prior model state uncertain. Unchanged warm continuations do not repeat the full snapshot.\n- Pi/OpenClaw persists a hidden runtime-generated current-turn carrier; Codex uses turn-scoped developer instructions; Copilot prefixes only the current turn. Reusable thread/system instructions stay stable.\n- Preflight, manual, queued, and direct compaction; queue draining; memory runs; Codex; Copilot; and fallback/recovery paths all carry the same effective policy.\n- The existing native GPT-5.5/GPT-5.6 Responses continuation fix remains narrow: built-in authenticated streams can use `previous_response_id` plus delta input, while custom streams keep their existing routing.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers should get materially better warm-turn cache reuse without weakening room-event delivery or sender authorization.\n\nOwner-authorized direct turns can still execute protected tools. Ambient room events and unauthorized/unknown senders still cannot execute those tools, even though their schemas remain visible for cache stability. A user message cannot grant access because authorization is checked again immediately before execution.\n\nQueueing, memory maintenance, compaction, and runtime fallback no longer silently discard the active policy or rebuild from the wrong delivery mode.\n\n## Evidence\n\nSanitized same-session traces from the original Discord reproduction showed:\n\n- prompt-visible inventory flipping between 44 and 47 tools;\n- matching system-prompt hash changes;\n- cached prefixes collapsing to roughly 2k-10k tokens or ceilinging around 37k-40k;\n- after stabilizing prompt/tool shape, warm turns reaching roughly 49k-52k cached tokens on approximately 51k-55k context.\n\nThe latest head, `be9ef558a98035f3d2f85f575d763dc09ba53276`, adds the turn-scoped policy and hard runtime boundary while preserving that stable schema shape. Tests prove:\n\n- owner direct, owner room-event, non-owner room-event, and return-to-owner turns serialize identical ordered tool names, descriptions, and JSON schemas;\n- policy versions change independently of schemas when sender/event access changes;\n- all four protected tools remain described but denied calls cannot reach hooks or implementations, including repeated and spoof-like calls;\n- a permitted owner turn still reaches the original protected-tool implementation;\n- the full snapshot precedes current user content, is not repeated on unchanged continuation, and is restored after reconstruction, queue rebuilds, memory runs, fallback, recovery, and every compaction entry point;\n- Codex policy changes are current-turn developer instructions, never reusable thread instructions;\n- Copilot policy changes are current-turn prompt content, never reusable system instructions;\n- targetless strict room-event sends still reach the internal source-reply sink, and private final text does not become visible;\n- GPT-5.5 and GPT-5.6 second turns use `previous_response_id` with delta-only input; a changed policy is part of that delta; custom streams are not reclassified.\n\nValidation on the exact head:\n\n- changed regression set: 34 files, 1,231 tests passed;\n- final focused policy/schema/trust check: 5 files, 169 tests passed;\n- `pnpm tsgo:prod` passed;\n- `pnpm check:test-types` passed;\n- `pnpm tsgo:test:packages` passed;\n- `pnpm lint:core` and `pnpm lint:extensions` passed;\n- scoped `oxfmt --check` and `git diff --check` passed;\n- extension package boundary compile passed for 120 plugins and 2 canaries;\n- `pnpm build` passed;\n- exact head installed locally: LaunchAgent loaded, gateway running, RPC probe OK, port 18789 listening, dashboard HTTP 200.\n\n## Compatibility And Security Boundaries\n\n- This does not expose every installed plugin. It stabilizes the session/runtime superset that existing construction policy already makes available.\n- Prompt text is advisory. The outer runtime wrapper is the authorization boundary and preserves existing tool-specific checks on allowed calls.\n- Pi's generic LLM message contract has no developer role, so its trusted policy carrier is a hidden runtime-generated current-turn custom message that projects to model input; it is not a fake tool result. Runtime authorization does not trust that message.\n- No sender IDs, channel IDs, credentials, hosts, or private trace payloads appear in policy snapshots or evidence.\n- No persistent data migration is required.\n- The branch is intentionally not rebased on today's fast-moving `main`; maintainers can refresh it during merge preparation.\n\nDisclaimer: This PR was filed and updated autonomously by Codex on behalf of the reporter. Private user, channel, host, credential, and full trace identifiers were removed from the evidence.\n",
